<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Example patch file for modpack authors.
  Put a file named bellum_feudal_titles_patch.xml in another module's ModuleData folder.
  Bellum will load it after its main feudal title config.

  Reusing the same title id overwrites that configured title definition.
  Reusing the same TitleStyle culture/kingdom merges it rank by rank and field
  by field. Omitted ranks and fields retain the value loaded before this patch.

  Selectable terminology presets use bellum_title_styles_*.xml instead. Their
  load order is main hierarchy, selected style preset, then every compatibility
  patch in active module order. Later patches win, including over this one.

  Presets and patches may rename an individual title without redefining its
  structure:
    <TitleName id="duchy_ebor" name="Ebor" />

  Title name is the territorial root/custom name. TitleStyle decides how that
  root is rendered by culture or kingdom:
    name="the Tall Hills" + Vlandian County style => County of the Tall Hills
    holder display => Count of the Tall Hills

  Optional rank formats can change word order per culture/kingdom:
    titleFormat="{TITLE_NAME} {TITLE_NOUN}"      => Jaculan County
    heldTitleFormat="{TITLE_NAME} {TITLE_RANK}"  => Jaculan Count

  Recruited wanderers receive no honorific unless the applicable Rank adds:
    wandererRank="Retainer" femaleWandererRank="Retainer"

  Optional landless noble house-leader honorifics on the sovereign Rank tier:
    landlessLeaderRank="Patriarch" femaleLandlessLeaderRank="Matriarch"
  These apply only to adult noble clan leaders in a realm with no active
  de facto title. Royal/heir/consort styles retain priority. Omit both to
  keep the ordinary noble honorific; one gender can serve as fallback for both.
  Like nobleRank, own-culture styles are preferred over realm fallbacks.

  Optional mercenary/minor-faction leader honorifics on Rank tier="Kingdom":
    mercenaryLeaderRank="Captain" femaleMercenaryLeaderRank="Captain"
  Own-culture style first, realm fallback; works between contracts as well.
  Only adult company leaders qualify (including a hired player); bandits,
  notables, ordinary members and spouses do not. Omitted fields retain names.
-->
<BellumFeudalTitles>
  <!--
    Starting clientage examples for conversion/map mods (all commented out).
    Add entries directly under BellumFeudalTitles. Use exact, case-sensitive
    kingdom IDs, not title IDs or translated names. Applied only to NEW games
    after campaign initialization; existing saves and later independence stay
    untouched. Title hierarchy and clan ownership are not changed.

    A later entry replaces the entire earlier entry for the same client:
    <StartingClientage client="border_principality" suzerain="great_empire"
                      submission="Voluntary" />
    <StartingClientage client="conquered_kingdom" suzerain="great_empire"
                      submission="Forced" liberationCooldownDays="100" />

    Required submission: Voluntary or Forced (normal Liberty Desire rules).
    Optional liberationCooldownDays: nonnegative whole days, default 0.
    This only delays liberation; it does NOT make the clientage expire.
    Omitting it in an override resets the configured initial lock to 0.
    Lawfulness comes separately from the de jure hierarchy.

    To cancel a declaration made by an earlier-loaded module:
    <StartingClientage client="border_principality" remove="true" />

    Each client has one suzerain; several clients may share that suzerain.
    Self-clientage, chains/cycles, and pre-existing clientage conflicts are
    rejected. Invalid kingdom IDs or graph conflicts stop the startup batch;
    malformed entries are logged and ignored (they also discard an earlier
    declaration for the same client). Check Bellum's log for the source file.
    Clients follow their suzerain's starting wars and are at peace within the
    bloc. Independent client wars and incompatible diplomacy are cleared,
    then protected alliance/trade agreements are established with the suzerain.
    Use this patch filename, not a bellum_title_styles_*.xml terminology preset.
    Restart the game after editing configuration.
  -->
  <!--
  <TitleStyle culture="Vlandia">
    <CouncilOffices name="Privy Council" marshal="Marshal" chancellor="Chancellor" seneschal="Seneschal" spymaster="Spymaster" firstAdvisor="First Advisor" secondAdvisor="Second Advisor" />
    <Rank tier="Barony" titleName="Barony" maleRank="Baron" femaleRank="Baroness" />
    <Rank tier="County" titleName="County" maleRank="Count" femaleRank="Countess" />
    <Rank tier="Duchy" titleName="Duchy" maleRank="Duke" femaleRank="Duchess" />
    <Rank tier="Kingdom" titleName="Kingdom" maleRank="King" femaleRank="Queen" heirRank="Crown Prince" femaleHeirRank="Crown Princess" childRank="Prince" femaleChildRank="Princess" nobleRank="Lord" femaleNobleRank="Lady" />
    <Rank tier="Empire" titleName="Empire" maleRank="Emperor" femaleRank="Empress" heirRank="Crown Prince" femaleHeirRank="Crown Princess" childRank="Prince" femaleChildRank="Princess" nobleRank="Lord" femaleNobleRank="Lady" />
  </TitleStyle>

  <Title id="county_custom_sargot" type="County" name="the Tall Hills" capital="Sargot" deJureClan="dey Meroc" kingdom="Vlandia">
    <Child settlement="Sargot" />
    <Child settlement="Talivel Castle" />
    <Child settlement="Thractorae Castle" />
  </Title>

  <TitleStyle culture="example_culture">
    <Rank tier="County" titleName="County" maleRank="Count" femaleRank="Countess" titleFormat="{TITLE_NAME} {TITLE_NOUN}" heldTitleFormat="{TITLE_NAME} {TITLE_RANK}" />
  </TitleStyle>
  -->
</BellumFeudalTitles>
